{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The proposed diagnostic—justification-evidence linkage in logs—fails to distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere compliance in the single evaluation the problem asks about. A panel can be trained to quote the exact case data point that satisfies a checklist item ('I found X which satisfies the box') without that linkage representing genuine reliability rather than compliance: if the checklist item itself prescribes which evidence to look for, then producing the matching evidence is just a more verbose form of box-ticking. The target's own countercondition (faked justifications copying text without understanding) concedes the diagnostic is spoofable, but the core argument does not show how a single evaluation would separate a panel that understands the linkage from one that merely copies the prescribed text. This is a counterexample-driven gap: the distinguishing evidence the target claims to identify does not, by construction, distinguish what the problem requires.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Checklist item says: 'Verify the widget voltage rating is ≥ 240V.' Panel writes: 'The label states 250V, satisfying ≥240V.' This is justification-evidence linkage per the target, yet if the checklist told them exactly which datum to retrieve, the linkage is mechanically produced compliance, not independent reliability. A single evaluation with such items cannot tell genuine gain from compliant tracking."}],"target":"2bbfee265cf821d844aa3b0c3f056bf7b1930a08d3139eab222479479b4e2707"},{"attack":true,"case":"The 'Goldilocks zone' claim answers a different question than the one posed. The problem asks for evidence within a single evaluation that distinguishes genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. SRC_002 instead offers a design condition for when a checklist could be reliable (granularity must avoid cognitive collapse). But knowing that granularity is in some optimal range does not, in a single evaluation, separate a panel that genuinely verified from one that compliantly tracked the correct answer while inside that same range. The mechanism (cognitive load optimization) predicts whether compliance fatigue is avoided, not whether the observed correct outputs are genuine versus coincidental. Thus the target fails to identify the distinguishing evidence the problem explicitly requires.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Two panels use the same Goldilocks-granularity checklist and both get the right answer. One panel reasoned through each item; the other guessed-and-checked then aligned justifications to fit. Since granularity is identical and optimal for both, the Goldilocks criterion cannot distinguish genuine reliability from compliance in the single evaluation."}],"target":"57333a3463549384e902a75cb5783013bfab6613261274a6c8861bea7f5e1ad8"},{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_003 argues the panel is more reliable by replacing high-variance expert peaks with a high-floor baseline, but it does not establish that this higher floor is genuine reliability rather than mere compliance that tracks the right answer on the evaluated items. Raising the floor only shows the panel avoids catastrophic slips; it does not, within a single evaluation, distinguish a panel that genuinely verified each item from one that compliance-ticked the same items and happened to be correct because the checklist happened to match the outcome. The problem explicitly demands this distinction, and the variance-reduction mechanism does not supply it: low variance can come from uniform compliance just as easily as from uniform genuine verification. The analogy to surgical safety checklists does not close the gap because the target itself flags that those checklists are used by experts, not non-experts, so the transfer claim that the floor-raising is genuine (not mere compliance) is unsupported for the non-expert panel the problem concerns.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A non-expert panel using the checklist achieves uniformly correct answers with low variance by mechanically satisfying each item without understanding. A single evaluation comparing them to a high-variance expert would show the panel has a higher floor, yet that floor could be compliance tracking the right answer rather than genuine reliability—exactly the confound the problem asks to distinguish."}],"target":"ce435db108fe60cf8094607c89b98507da224e3156505ea316132f37c9b4fa15"}]}